doc_id: api-design-patterns
title: API Design Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: rest_api_standards
description: Central standards for REST API design, including URL structure, request/response schemas, error responses, and OpenAPI documentation.
tech_stack: Captures stable 2024-2025 FastAPI and Pydantic v2 practices for this backend.
scope: This file is the source of truth for API-wide behavior and contracts. Router composition, service and repository patterns, and tests are covered separately in router-patterns.toon, service-patterns.toon, and dependency-patterns.md.
primary_runtime_references[4]:
  - path: app/core/factory.py
    role: FastAPI application construction, default response class, and custom OpenAPI schema.
  - path: app/core/exceptions.py
    role: Global request validation handler.
  - path: app/api/v1/router.py
    role: Versioned API router registration.
  - path: app/contracts/errors.py
    role: HTTP validation error contracts.
related_pattern_docs[3]:
  - router-patterns.toon
  - service-patterns.toon
  - dependency-patterns.md
sections[8]:
  - id: url-structure-versioning
    index: 1
    title: URL structure and versioning
    category: standard
    summary: URL path conventions, version prefixes, health checks, and resource naming.
    items[6]:
      - id: url.prefix
        type: rule
        text: "Mount all versioned endpoints under /api/{version} (for example /api/v1) using create_app in app/core/factory.py."
      - id: url.health.liveness
        type: rule
        text: Expose GET /health as an unversioned liveness probe, exclude it from OpenAPI, and let orchestrators use it to check process aliveness.
      - id: url.health.readiness
        type: rule
        text: "Expose GET /api/{version}/health as the versioned readiness endpoint, register it in the versioned API router, and document it in OpenAPI."
      - id: url.resource-naming
        type: rule
        text: Use plural, kebab-case resource names in paths such as /api/v1/task-assignments and /api/v1/user-sessions.
      - id: url.path-params
        type: rule
        text: "Use descriptive path parameter names like {user_id} instead of generic {id}."
      - id: url.trailing-slashes
        type: rule
        text: "Declare canonical paths without a trailing slash (for example /users and /users/{user_id}); FastAPI handling of .../ redirects is not part of the API contract."
  - id: http-methods-and-status-codes
    index: 2
    title: HTTP methods and status codes
    category: standard
    summary: Standard REST semantics plus a strict rule that validation failures are 400 Bad Request.
    http_method_defaults[5]:
      - method: GET
        usage: Retrieve a resource or list
        success_status: 200
        success_status_text: OK
        error_statuses[1]: 404
      - method: POST
        usage: Create a new resource
        success_status: 201
        success_status_text: Created
        error_statuses[2]: 400,409
      - method: PUT
        usage: Replace an entire resource
        success_status: 200
        success_status_text: OK
        error_statuses[2]: 400,404
      - method: PATCH
        usage: Partially update a resource
        success_status: 200
        success_status_text: OK
        error_statuses[2]: 400,404
      - method: DELETE
        usage: Delete a resource
        success_status: 204
        success_status_text: No Content
        error_statuses[1]: 404
    items[5]:
      - id: http.status.named-constants
        type: rule
        text: Use fastapi.status named constants such as status.HTTP_201_CREATED instead of hard-coded integer literals.
      - id: http.validation.use-400
        type: rule
        text: Surface all request validation failures as HTTP 400 Bad Request instead of the FastAPI default 422 for RequestValidationError.
      - id: http.validation.handler-registration
        type: note
        text: create_app registers validation_exception_handler from app/core/exceptions.py as the global handler for RequestValidationError.
      - id: http.validation.error-model
        type: note
        text: The validation exception handler returns a 400 response with an AppError envelope containing the HTTPValidationError payload in the details field. This ensures all error responses share the same top-level structure (code, message, statusCode, details).
      - id: http.validation.route-responses
        type: rule
        text: Route decorators must reuse VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE from app/contracts/errors.py in the responses mapping for 400 entries. This constant now references AppError for OpenAPI documentation consistency.
  - id: request-response-modeling
    index: 3
    title: Request and response modeling
    category: standard
    summary: Pydantic v2 models with extra fields forbidden, snake_case JSON, and explicit response models.
    sample_code:
      description: Generic FastAPI route example showing response_model and VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE usage.
      imports[4]: datetime.datetime,typing.Annotated,fastapi.APIRouter,pydantic.BaseModel
      models[2]:
        - name: ResourceCreate
          config: "ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")"
          fields[1]: "name: Annotated[str, Field(min_length=1)]"
        - name: ResourceResponse
          config: "ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")"
          fields[1]: "id: int,name: str,created_at: datetime"
      route:
        method: POST
        path: /resources
        response_model: ResourceResponse
        status_code: status.HTTP_201_CREATED
        summary: Create resource
        description: Creates a resource and returns its representation.
        responses: "400: VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE, 409: {\"description\": \"Conflict\"}"
    items[4]:
      - id: modeling.pydantic-extra-forbid
        type: rule
        text: "Define all request and response payloads as Pydantic BaseModel subclasses with model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\") to reject unknown JSON fields."
      - id: modeling.json-snake-case
        type: rule
        text: Use snake_case for all JSON field names; represent datetimes as timezone-aware UTC ISO 8601 strings.
      - id: modeling.response-model-required
        type: rule
        text: Every endpoint must set response_model so that outbound schemas are enforced, extra fields from services are filtered out, and OpenAPI schemas are derived from the contracts.
      - id: modeling.describe-side-effects
        type: rule
        text: When a route has non-trivial side effects such as writes or external calls, the description must explain these behaviors for API consumers.
  - id: openapi-documentation
    index: 4
    title: OpenAPI documentation standards
    category: standard
    summary: The generated OpenAPI schema is the external contract; every route must be documented consistently.
    items[7]:
      - id: openapi.summary-one-line
        type: rule
        text: Provide a short, action-oriented summary string for every route.
      - id: openapi.description-details
        type: rule
        text: Use description to document complex behavior, side effects, and edge cases; Markdown is allowed.
      - id: openapi.tags-domain
        type: rule
        text: "Group endpoints into domain-specific tags such as Users or Health (for example tags=[\"Users\"])."
      - id: openapi.errors.document-non-success
        type: rule
        text: Declare all non-success HTTP responses explicitly using the responses parameter on the route decorator.
      - id: openapi.errors.validation-contract
        type: rule
        text: For request validation errors, use VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE to document a 400 response using the AppError envelope as the top-level error schema. The HTTPValidationError payload containing field-level validation details is nested inside AppError.details.
      - id: openapi.errors.do-not-duplicate-schemas
        type: rule
        text: Rely on create_app to register both the AppError schema (used for all error responses) and the HTTPValidationError schema (for the nested validation structure) in the OpenAPI schema; do not duplicate these schema definitions manually.
      - id: openapi.consistency.router-level
        type: rule
        text: Keep router-specific shared documentation such as common responses at the router level, while ensuring they still satisfy these API-wide OpenAPI expectations.
  - id: response-shape-and-pagination
    index: 5
    title: Response shape and pagination
    category: standard
    summary: Successful responses return domain models directly; pagination and errors follow shared envelopes.
    pagination_fields[4]: items,total,page,page_size
    pagination_type_hints:
      items: "list[T]"
      total: int
      page: int
      page_size: int
    items[7]:
      - id: responses.no-generic-envelopes
        type: rule
        text: "Do not wrap successful responses in generic envelopes such as {\"status\": \"ok\", \"data\": ...}; return the domain model dictated by response_model."
      - id: pagination.standard-fields
        type: rule
        text: "For paginated collections, use a structure with items, total, page, and page_size fields until a generic Paginated[T] model exists under app/contracts/."
      - id: errors.app-error-envelope
        type: rule
        text: All error responses share a common top-level envelope using the AppError schema defined in app/contracts/errors.py with code, message, statusCode, and optional details fields.
      - id: errors.validation-through-app-error
        type: rule
        text: "Validation failures use the AppError envelope with code=\"VALIDATION_ERROR\" and the HTTPValidationError payload (containing field-level detail and optional body) placed in the details field. This provides a consistent top-level structure while preserving detailed validation information."
      - id: errors.non-validation-through-app-error
        type: rule
        text: Non-validation errors such as domain or infrastructure failures use the AppError envelope with domain-specific code values (from the ErrorCode enum) and contextual details where applicable.
      - id: errors.route-decorator-usage
        type: rule
        text: "Route decorators use VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE (which now references AppError) for 400 responses and {\"model\": AppError, \"description\": \"...\"} for other error status codes."
      - id: errors.todo.paginated-model
        type: todo
        text: "Once a generic Paginated[T] model exists under app/contracts/, update routers and this document to reference it as the canonical pagination schema."
  - id: content-negotiation
    index: 6
    title: Content negotiation
    category: standard
    summary: JSON plus ORJSONResponse by default, with gzip compression support.
    items[3]:
      - id: content.json-default
        type: rule
        text: "Use application/json as the default Content-Type unless a route explicitly declares a different media type."
      - id: content.serializer-orjson
        type: rule
        text: Configure ORJSONResponse as the default_response_class in create_app and avoid per-route overrides unless a different content type is required.
      - id: content.compression-gzip
        type: rule
        text: "Support Accept-Encoding: gzip via middleware and ensure compressed responses remain valid JSON at the payload level."
  - id: versioning-and-deprecation
    index: 7
    title: Versioning and deprecation
    category: standard
    summary: Path-based API versioning with additive-only changes per version and explicit deprecation metadata.
    items[7]:
      - id: versioning.path-based
        type: rule
        text: Version all public endpoints in the path such as /api/v1 or /api/v2, and require a new major version for any breaking change.
      - id: versioning.compatibility-additive
        type: rule
        text: Within a given API version, allow only additive changes such as new optional fields or new endpoints.
      - id: versioning.compatibility-no-breaking
        type: rule
        text: Do not remove fields or change semantics within an existing API version; such changes require a new version.
      - id: deprecation.flag-endpoints
        type: rule
        text: When deprecating an endpoint, set deprecated=True in the route decorator so OpenAPI and interactive docs show deprecation state.
      - id: deprecation.header-deprecation
        type: rule
        text: "For deprecated endpoints, include a Deprecation response header such as Deprecation: true or a deprecation date string."
      - id: deprecation.header-sunset
        type: rule
        text: "When a removal date is known for a deprecated endpoint, include a Sunset header with an RFC 1123 timestamp such as Mon, 30 Jun 2025 23:59:59 GMT."
      - id: deprecation.changelog
        type: rule
        text: Record every breaking or deprecating change in the API changelog with migration guidance to replacement endpoints or versions.
  - id: anti-patterns
    index: 8
    title: Anti-patterns to avoid
    category: anti_patterns
    summary: Common pitfalls that violate these standards.
    items[6]:
      - id: anti-patterns.business-logic-in-routes
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Implementing business logic directly in route handlers instead of delegating to services.
      - id: anti-patterns.orm-models-as-contracts
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Returning ORM or database models directly from endpoints instead of Pydantic contract models.
      - id: anti-patterns.inconsistent-error-shapes
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Using inconsistent or undocumented error response shapes across endpoints.
      - id: anti-patterns.missing-response-model
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Omitting response_model or leaving success and error status codes implicit.
      - id: anti-patterns.broad-except
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Catching broad exceptions such as except Exception in ways that hide root causes or bypass global exception handling.
      - id: anti-patterns.missing-openapi-docs
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Leaving OpenAPI documentation incomplete, for example by omitting summary, description, tags, or documented error responses.